# Manim Cheat Sheet

Oct 14, 2026 · @Mustafa

The mobjects, their parameters, and the animations plus self.play() options in Manim Community Edition. Parameter lists were checked against docs.manim.community (stable) on the date above. The few points that come from my own reasoning and not from the docs are marked as such.

## 1. Mobjects

Everything you put on screen is a mobject. This is not every mobject in Manim; the full list is the reference page on docs.manim.community.

| Group | Mobjects | Checked |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Circles and arcs | Circle, Arc, ArcBetweenPoints, Ellipse, Dot, AnnotationDot, LabeledDot, Annulus, AnnularSector, Sector, TangentialArc, CubicBezier, CurvedArrow, CurvedDoubleArrow | Yes |
| Polygons | Polygon, Polygram, RegularPolygon, RegularPolygram, Star, Triangle, Rectangle, Square, RoundedRectangle, ArcPolygon | Yes |
| Lines and arrows | Line, DashedLine, TangentLine, Elbow, Arrow, DoubleArrow, Vector, Angle, RightAngle | Yes |
| Text | Text, MarkupText, Paragraph | Yes |
| LaTeX text | Tex, MathTex | Yes |
| Images and files | ImageMobject (pixel image), SVGMobject (vector image) | Yes |
| Grouping | VGroup, Group | Yes |
| Graphs and axes | Axes, NumberPlane, NumberLine | Yes |
| Decoration | Brace, SurroundingRectangle | Yes |
| 3D (use inside a ThreeDScene) | Cube, Sphere, Cone, Cylinder, Torus, Prism, Surface, ThreeDAxes | Yes |

A mobject you build yourself from points is a VMobject. Most of the shapes, lines and text above are VMobjects, so they share the styling options in section 2. ImageMobject is not one.

## 2. Parameters

Each mobject has its own parameters, and then passes everything else (\*\*kwargs) up to its parent class. That is why Square(side\_length=4, fill\_color=BLUE) works: side\_length belongs to Square, and fill\_color travels up to VMobject.

### Shared styling options (VMobject)

| Parameter | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| fill\_color | None | Color inside the shape |
| fill\_opacity | 0.0 | 0 is see-through, 1 is solid |
| stroke\_color | None | Color of the outline |
| stroke\_opacity | 1.0 | Outline opacity |
| stroke\_width | 4 | Outline thickness |
| background\_stroke\_color | black | Color of an outline drawn behind the shape |
| background\_stroke\_opacity | 1.0 | Opacity of that background outline |
| background\_stroke\_width | 0 | Thickness of that background outline |
| sheen\_factor | 0.0 | Blends the color toward white along sheen\_direction |
| sheen\_direction | \[-1, 1, 0\] | Direction of the sheen |
| joint\_type | None | How curve segments join |
| cap\_style | AUTO | Shape of line ends |
| shade\_in\_3d | False | Shading in 3D scenes |

The docs also list close\_new\_points, tolerance\_for\_point\_equality, n\_points\_per\_cubic\_curve, background\_image, pre\_function\_handle\_to\_anchor\_scale\_factor and make\_smooth\_after\_applying\_functions. You will rarely need them.

