Two animations on the same mobject in one self.play() can fight each other, because each one rewrites that mobject every frame (my understanding of how Manim works, not checked in the docs). Play them one after another, or put both inside one .animate chain.

### The .animate attribute

.animate is not an animation. It turns a mobject method into one, so the method's own arguments apply:

- circ.animate.shift(UP) moves it.
- circ.animate.next\_to(txt, RIGHT, buff=2) moves it next to another mobject.
- square.animate.shift(UP).rotate(PI / 3) chains two changes into one animation. This example is in the Manim docs.
- You can pass options to .animate itself. Manim's own docs show group.animate(lag\_ratio=ratio, run\_time=1.5).shift(DOWN \* 2). They do not show rate\_func used this way.
- Animation classes do not go after .animate. Write DrawBorderThenFill(banana), not banana.animate.DrawBorderThenFill.
- A full 2\*PI turn through .animate.rotate may look like nothing, because the start and end shapes match (my own reasoning, not checked). Use Rotate or Rotating for full spins.

### self.play() parameters

| Parameter | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| \*args | none | The animations to play: an Animation, a Mobject, or a .animate expression. Several in one call play together |
| subcaption | None | Subtitle text shown during the animation |
| subcaption\_duration | None | How long the subtitle shows. None uses the animation's run time |
| subcaption\_offset | 0 | Seconds added to the subtitle's start time |
| \*\*kwargs | none | Copied onto every animation in the call, as in self.play(Create(c), run\_time=2). run\_time and rate\_func are the usual ones. lag\_ratio only matters for animations that have parts |

Other scene methods:

- self.wait(duration, stop\_condition=None, frozen\_frame=None) pauses. The default for duration is a constant called DEFAULT\_WAIT\_TIME, and the page I opened did not show its value, so pass a number to be sure.
- self.add(\*mobjects) puts mobjects on screen with no animation.
- self.remove(\*mobjects) takes them off screen with no animation.

